"Identification Requirements | Department of Public Safety (https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/identification-requirements)\nciteturn19view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/identification-requirements\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 129\nL0: Search Form Search\nL1: \nL2: [Input: What can we help you find?]\nL3: \nL4: [Button: Search]\nL5: \nL6: [Input] [Input]\nL7: \nL8: ## Change Text Size\nL9: \nL10: ^{-}A A ^{+}A\nL11: \nL12: ## Change Display Setting\nL13: \nL14: cite0†Default cite1†Text Only L15: \nL16: # Identification Requirements\nL17: \nL18: September 22, 2020\nL19: ###\nL20: \nL21: Individuals must complete additional requirements to obtain a cite2†driver license or cite3†ID card .\nL22: \nL23: To help reduce identity theft, individuals must present documents to verify their identity when applying for a driver license or identification card.\nL24: \nL25: There are three ways an individual can verify his/her identity:\nL26:   1. Bring one item listed in the “Primary Identity Documents” category or\nL27:   2. Bring two items listed in the “Secondary Identity Documents” category or\nL28:   3. Bring one item listed in the “Secondary Identity Documents” category and two documents listed in the “Supporting Identity Documents” category\nL29: The information on the documents, such as name and date of birth, must all match. If the name is different on each document, then the individual must provide documents that verify a legal name change. If other information on the document is different, speak with a License and Permit Specialist for assistance.\nL30: If you are using a name other than what is on your birth certificate, (example: married name), you will be required to show legal documentation of name change. Documents must be original or certified copy. Certified documents should not be laminated. No photocopies can be accepted.\nL31: \nL32: Acceptable documents:\nL33:   1. Marriage license\nL34:   2. Divorce decree\nL35:   3. Original or certified copy of a revised birth certificate\nL36:   4. Court ordered name change\nL37:   5. Department of State Health Services marriage verification letter\nL38: \nL39: NOTE: A cite4†brochure listing all of the following documents is available for download.\nL40: \nL41: \nL42: Primary Identity Documents\nL43: \nL44: An individual can present one primary document. No other documents are required to verify identity if one primary document is provided.\nL45: \nL46: Primary identity documents include:\nL47:   * Texas driver license or Texas identification card not expired more than 2 years\nL48:     * cite5†Proof of citizenship will be required if not previously established\nL49:   * Valid, unexpired U.S. passport book or U.S. passport card\nL50:   * U.S. Certificate of Citizenship or Certificate of Naturalization with identifiable photograph (N-550, N-560, N-561, N-570 or N-578)\nL51:   * Unexpired Department of Homeland Security or U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services document with verifiable data and identifiable photo, such as one of the following:\nL52:     1. U.S. Citizen Identification Card (I-179 or I-197)\nL53:     2. Permanent Resident Card (I-551)\nL54:     3. Machine Readable Immigrant Visa with temporary I-551 language and ADIT stamp\nL55:     4. Employment Authorization Card (I-766)\nL56:     5. Unexpired U.S. Travel Document (I-327 or I-571)\nL57:     6. Advance Parole Document (I-512 or I-512L)\nL58:     7. I-94 stamped Sec. 208 Asylee with photo\nL59:     8. I-94 stamped Sec. 207 Refugee with photo\nL60:     9. Valid Refugee Travel Letter with photo, stamped by Customs and Border Protection\nL61:     10. American Indian Card (I-872)\nL62:     11. Northern Mariana card (I-873)\nL63:   * Valid, unexpired foreign passport with attached visa (the visa requirement may be waived in the case of Parolees, the Republic of Palau, the Republic of the Marshall Islands, the Federated States of Micronesia and certain Canadian non-immigrants) and valid Form I-94\nL64:   * Unexpired U.S. military ID card for active duty, reserve or retired personnel with identifiable photo\nL65: \nL66: Secondary Identity Documents\nL67: \nL68: Individuals who do not have a primary document can present two secondary documents. No other documents are required to verify identity if two secondary documents are provided.\nL69: \nL70: Secondary identity documents include:\nL71:   * Original or certified copy of a birth certificate issued by a State Bureau of Vital Statistics or equivalent agency from a U.S. state, U.S. territory, the District of Columbia or a Canadian province\nL72:     * Laminated copies are not accepted\nL73:     * No photocopies can be accepted\nL74:   * Original or certified copy of a court order with name and date of birth indicating an official change of name from a U.S. state, U.S. territory, the District of Columbia or a Canadian province\nL75: NOTE: Because Puerto Rican statute provides that Puerto Rican birth certificates issued before July 1, 2010 are no longer valid, the Department cannot recognize these birth certificates as proof of identification or lawful presence. For more information please select the following link. cite6†http://prfaa.pr.gov/birthcertificatesnd2.asp†prfaa.pr.gov L76:   * For U.S. citizens born abroad - Original or certified copy of Consular report of Birth Abroad (CRBA) (Form FS-240) or Certificate of Report of Birth (DS-1350 for FS-545) issued by the U.S. Department of State\nL77: \nL78: Birth documents other than the secondary identity documents listed above, may be accepted as a secondary identity document, provided it meets the following minimum requirements:\nL79:   * Issued by a local or state government\nL80:   * Lists bearer's full name, date of birth, gender, and place of birth\nL81:   * Lists parent(s) full names\nL82:   * Has date filed with registrar's office\nL83:   * Has registrar's signature\nL84:   * Has embossed, impressed, or multicolored seal of registrar\nL85: \nL86: Supporting Identity Documents\nL87: \nL88: Individuals who do not have a primary document or two secondary documents can provide one secondary document (from the list above) and two supporting documents.\nL89: Supporting identity documents include:\nL90:   * Social security card (actual card)\nL91:   * Form W-2 or 1099\nL92:   * Driver license or ID card issued by another U.S. state, U.S. territory, the District of Columbia or Canadian province (unexpired or expired less than two years)*\nL93:   * Texas driver license or ID card that has been expired more than two years (actual card)\nL94:   * Temporary Texas driver license or ID card (actual receipt)\nL95:   * School records* (e.g., report cards, photo ID cards)\nL96:   * Unexpired U.S. military dependent identification card\nL97:   * Original or certified copy of marriage license or divorce decree (if the document is not in English, a certified translation must accompany it)\nL98:   * Valid, unexpired Texas Voter registration card*\nL99:   * Pilot license* (actual card)\nL100:   * Concealed handgun license or License to Carry*\nL101:   * Professional license issued by a Texas state agency\nL102:   * ID card issued by a government agency*\nL103:   * A valid consular document issued by a state or national government\nL104:   * Texas Inmate ID card or similar form of ID issued by Texas Department of Criminal Justice\nL105:   * Texas Department of Criminal Justice parole or mandatory release certificate\nL106:   * Federal inmate identification card\nL107:   * Federal parole or release certificate\nL108:   * Medicare or Medicaid card (actual card)\nL109:   * Selective Service card (actual card)\nL110:   * Immunization records*\nL111:   * Tribal membership card from federally-recognized tribe\nL112:   * Certificate of Degree of Indian Blood\nL113:   * Valid, unexpired foreign passport\nL114:   * Insurance policy valid continuously for the past two years (e.g., auto, home or life insurance)\nL115:   * Valid, unexpired Texas vehicle registration or title\nL116:   * Valid, unexpired Texas boat registration or title\nL117:   * Veteran's ID card issued by the U.S. Department of Veteran Affairs\nL118:   * Original or certified copy of an abstract (shortened) birth certificate issued by a State Bureau of Vital Statistics or equivalent agency from a U.S. state, U.S. territory, or the District of Columbia\nL119:   * Hospital-issued birth record*\nL120:   * Military records (e.g., Form DD-214, DD-215, NGB-22)\nL121: *The document must be issued by an institution, entity or government from a U.S. state, a U.S. territory, the District of Columbia or a Canadian province.\nL122: ### Follow DPS\nL123: \nL124: ### Keep Texas Safe\nL125: \nL126: cite7†Report Suspicious Activity†iwatchtx.org L127: \nL128: © 2026 Texas Department of Public Safety. PDF files require cite8†Adobe Reader†get.adobe.com or compatible.\n"
